00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:05.940
Sentiment classification is a task of looking at a piece of text and telling if someone

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.900
likes or dislikes the thing they are talking about.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:14.340
It is one of the most important building blocks in NLP and is used in many applications.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.180
One of the challenges of sentiment classification is you might not have a huge labeled training

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.380
set for it, but with word embeddings, you build good sentiment classifiers even with

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.700
only modest-sized labeled training sets.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.860
Let's see how you can do that.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.020
Here's an example of a sentiment classification problem.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:39.100
The input X is a piece of text and the output Y they want to predict is what is the sentiment

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.740
such as the star rating of let's say a restaurant review.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.780
Someone says the dessert is excellent and they give it a four-star review, service quite

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.900
slow, two-star review, good for a quick meal but nothing special, three-star review and

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.140
this is a pretty harsh review, complete lacking good taste, good service and good ambience

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.300
as a one-star review.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:03.220
If you can train a system to map from X to Y based on a labeled data set like this, then

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:08.420
you could use it to monitor comments that people are saying about maybe a restaurant

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.420
that you run.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.340
People might also post messages about your restaurant on social media, on Twitter or

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.780
Facebook or Instagram or other forms of social media.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.060
If you have a sentiment classifier, they can look just a piece of text and figure

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:28.180
out how possible negative is a sentiment of the poster to your restaurant, then you

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.900
can also keep track of whether or not there are any problems or if your restaurant is

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.940
getting better or worse over time.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:42.540
One of the challenges of sentiment classification is you might not have a huge label of data

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.540
set.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:49.220
If a sentiment classification task training sets with maybe anywhere from 10,000 to maybe

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:56.660
100,000 words would not be uncommon, sometimes even smaller than 10,000 words and word embeddings

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:02.500
that you can take can help you do much better on this task, especially when you have a small

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.500
training set.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.820
So, here's what you can do.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.820
We'll go through a couple of different algorithms in this video.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.260
Here's a simple sentiment classification model.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.260
You can take a sentence like, desert is excellent and look up those words in your

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.260
dictionary.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.820
This is 10,000 words actually as usual.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:25.420
Let's build a classifier to map it to the output Y that this was four stars.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:32.820
Given these four words, as usual, we can take these four words and look up the one

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:33.820
heart vector.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:34.820
So, that's 0, 8, 9, 2, 8.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.980
It's a one heart vector multiplied by the embedding matrix E, which you could learn

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.620
from a much larger task corpus.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:47.220
You can learn an embedding from say a billion words or 100 billion words and use that to

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:53.180
extract out the embedding vector for the word E and then do the same for desert, do the

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.180
same for is and do the same for excellent.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:04.940
And if this was trained on a very large dataset, like 100 billion words, then this allows

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:10.100
you to take a lot of knowledge even from infrequent words and apply them to your problem,

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.540
even words that weren't in your labeled training set.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:19.500
Now, here's one way to build a classifier, which is that you can take these vectors,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:28.210
let's say these are 300 dimensional vectors, and you could then just sum or average them.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:34.770
And I'm just going to put a bigger average operator here and you could use sum or average.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:43.050
This gives you a 300 dimensional feature vector that you then pass to a softmax classifier,

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.410
which then outputs y hat.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:50.090
And so the softmax can output what are the probabilities of the five possible outcomes

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:52.250
from one star up to five star.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:58.090
So this would be a softmax over five possible outcomes to predict what is y.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:04.290
So notice that by using the average operation here, this particular algorithm works for reviews

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:09.170
that are short or long, because even if you review that is 100 words long, you can just

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:12.530
sum or average all the feature vectors for 100 words.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:18.490
And that gives you a representation, a 300 dimensional feature representation that you

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:21.970
can then pass into your softmax classifier.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.010
So this algorithm will work decently well.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:29.730
And what it does is it really averages the meanings of all the words or sums the meaning

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:33.410
of all the words in your example.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.330
But this won't work too badly.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:38.810
So one of the problems with this algorithm is it ignores words order.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:43.410
In particular, this is a very negative review, completely lacking good taste, good service

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:44.570
and good ambience.

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.770
But the word good appears a lot.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.770
This is wow, good, good, good.

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:54.050
So if you use an algorithm like this that ignores word order, and just sums or averages

00:04:54.050 --> 00:05:00.130
all of the embeddings for the different words, then you end up having a lot of the representation

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.290
of good in your final feature vector.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:06.530
And your classifier will probably think this is a good review, even though this is actually

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:09.290
a very harsh, this is a one star review.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:13.890
So here's a more sophisticated model, which is that instead of just summing all of your

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:20.650
word embeddings, you can instead use a RNN for sentiment classification.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:21.650
So here's what you can do.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:26.490
You can take that review, completely lacking good taste, good service and good ambience,

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:30.170
and find for each of them the one-hot vector.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:34.130
And so I'm going to just skip the one-hot vector representation, but take the one-hot

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:40.730
vectors, multiply it by the embedding matrix E as usual, then this gives you the embedding

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.940
vectors.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:49.020
And then you can feed these into an RNN.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:55.140
And the job of the RNN is to then compute a representation at the last time step that

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:57.660
allows you to predict Y hat.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:07.260
So this is an example of a many-to-one RNN architecture, which we saw in the previous week.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:12.660
And with an algorithm like this, it will be much better at taking word sequence into account

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:17.460
and realize that things like lacking in good taste is a negative review and not good is

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.980
a negative review, unlike the previous algorithm, which just sums everything together into a

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:29.260
big word vector mush and doesn't realize that not good has a very different meaning

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:35.020
than the word good or lacking in good taste and so on.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:39.460
And so if you train this algorithm, you end up with a pretty decent sentiment classification

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:41.260
algorithm.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:45.900
And because your word embeddings can be trained from a much larger data set, this will do

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.020
a better job generalizing to maybe even new words now that you see in your training

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:56.340
set, such as if someone else is completely absent of good taste, good service and good

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:02.900
ambience or something, then even if the word absent is not in your labeled training set,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:08.940
if it was in your 1 billion or 100 billion word corpus used to train the word embeddings,

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:14.660
it might still get this right and generalize much better even to words that were in the

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:18.900
training set used to train the word embeddings, but not necessarily in the labeled training

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:23.420
set that you had for specifically the sentiment classification problem.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.300
So that's it for sentiment classification.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:31.020
I hope this gives you a sense of how once you've learned or downloaded from online a word

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:36.020
embedding, this allows you to quite quickly build pretty effective NLP systems.
